#232 -- pin the console-log PATH (not the descriptor), and stop discarding
the open/write results.

Sys_DebugLog (the -condebug sink, reached from Con_Printf/Con_DPrintf)
was:

    fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
    write(fd, data, strlen(data));
    close(fd);

with none of the three results checked. Two separate things are wrong,
and only one of them is the obvious one.

(1) THE PATH. The caller builds it as `va("%s/qconsole.log",
com_gamedir)`, and com_gamedir is EMPTY for most of a run -- populated
only briefly around COM_AddGameDirectory. So the path alternates between
a usable "<gamedir>/qconsole.log" and a bare "/qconsole.log" at the
filesystem root, which an unprivileged process cannot create. Unchecked,
every line emitted outside that brief window was dropped in silence.

MEASURED on Thylacine (thyla-pi KVM): after deleting the log and proving
it absent, a 969-frame timedemo left it holding exactly 47 bytes --
"Added packfile /quake/id1/pak0.pak (339 files)\n", the single line
printed while com_gamedir was set -- while the SAME Con_Printf calls
reached stdout for the whole 27-second run (Con_Printf does both in one
call, common/console.c:324). Listing /, /quake, /quake/id1 and the cwd
found no second qconsole.log: the output was dropped, not misfiled. The
engine's own errno, once it was made to report one, is EACCES on
"/qconsole.log".

(2) THE CLOSE IS LOAD-BEARING -- it is not churn to optimise away. It is
what PUBLISHES each line to other processes. Thylacine's guest FS cache
is close-to-open (I-38): a reader's view is fixed at its own open, so a
writer holding a descriptor is invisible to anyone else however much it
writes. An earlier revision of this patch held the descriptor and left
the file reading 0 BYTES to a separate reader -- which is exactly the
consumer this log exists for. The close stays; only the path is pinned.

The failure was invisible from both ends: the engine reported success it
never checked, and a reader of the log sees a healthy run as an infinite
hang.

The report is per-DISTINCT-PATH rather than one-shot, because a single
latch is spent by the harmless empty-gamedir failure and then hides any
later one that matters. A successful open announces its path once, so
"which file am I even writing?" is answerable from the console instead of
by rebuilding -- which is what caught a stale-staged binary during this
work.

Not sent upstream: a descriptor per line, and an empty gamedir aiming at
/, are both harmless on a host where the process can create files at the
root -- which is every platform upstream targets.

--- a/common/sys_unix.c	2026-08-13 13:28:18
+++ b/common/sys_unix.c	2026-08-13 13:28:46
@@ -274,13 +274,62 @@
 {
     va_list argptr;
     static char data[MAX_PRINTMSG];
+    static char good[1024];       /* the first path that actually worked */
+    static char refused[1024];    /* last path reported, to report each once */
+    static int wr_complained = 0;
+    const char *target;
     int fd;
 
     va_start(argptr, fmt);
     qvsnprintf(data, sizeof(data), fmt, argptr);
     va_end(argptr);
-    fd = open(file, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
-    write(fd, data, strlen(data));
+
+    /* Remember the PATH that worked -- not the descriptor.
+       Two separate things are wrong with the original, and only one of them
+       is the obvious one.
+
+       (1) The caller builds the path as `va("%s/qconsole.log", com_gamedir)`,
+       and com_gamedir is EMPTY for most of a run -- populated only briefly
+       around COM_AddGameDirectory. So the path alternates between a usable
+       "<gamedir>/qconsole.log" and a bare "/qconsole.log" at the filesystem
+       root, which an unprivileged process cannot create (EACCES). With none
+       of open/write/close ever checked, every line emitted outside that brief
+       window was discarded in silence. Pinning the first path that WORKS is
+       the fix for that.
+
+       (2) The open/write/CLOSE per line is NOT churn to be optimised away --
+       it is what PUBLISHES each line to other processes. Thylacine's guest FS
+       cache is close-to-open (I-38): a reader's view is fixed at its own open,
+       so a writer holding a descriptor makes its data invisible to anyone
+       else no matter how much it writes. An earlier attempt at this patch held
+       the descriptor open and left the file reading 0 bytes to a separate
+       reader -- which is precisely the consumer this log exists for (a bench
+       harness polling for the timedemo's fps line). So the close STAYS. */
+    target = good[0] ? good : file;
+
+    fd = open(target, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0666);
+    if (fd < 0) {
+        /* Report each DISTINCT path once. A one-shot latch would be spent by
+           the harmless empty-gamedir failure and then hide any later one that
+           matters -- an instrument disarmed by the thing it watches. */
+        if (strcmp(refused, target) != 0) {
+            strncpy(refused, target, sizeof(refused) - 1);
+            refused[sizeof(refused) - 1] = '\0';
+            fprintf(stderr, "Sys_DebugLog: cannot open %s: %s\n",
+                    target, strerror(errno));
+        }
+        return;
+    }
+    if (!good[0]) {
+        strncpy(good, target, sizeof(good) - 1);
+        good[sizeof(good) - 1] = '\0';
+        fprintf(stderr, "Sys_DebugLog: logging to %s\n", good);
+    }
+    if (write(fd, data, strlen(data)) < 0 && !wr_complained) {
+        wr_complained = 1;
+        fprintf(stderr, "Sys_DebugLog: write to %s failed: %s\n",
+                good, strerror(errno));
+    }
     close(fd);
 }
 
